edia": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-06T18:18:42Z", "username": "Abhishek Saluja", "text": ",but the late worm escapes should be the entire proverb", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-06T18:18:54Z", "username": "Abhishek Vats", "text": "Last question", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-06T18:20:09Z", "username": "Abhishek Vats", "text": "Another one inspired by KP's set from today", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-06T18:20:18Z", "username": "Abhishek Vats", "text": "13. The 1960 Tony Award for Best Musical remains Broadway’s most legendary deadlock, representing a literal tie between the \"Old Guard\" and the \"New Wave\" of musical theater. The race pitted the sentimental, record-breaking juggernaut X by Rodgers & Hammerstein against the gritty, fast-paced political satire \"Y!\" by Bock & Harnick. While the former captured the hearts of the public with its lush score and themes of faith, the latter won over the critics with its sharp wit and intelligence, eventually earning a Pulitzer Prize. Because the voting body was split precisely down the middle between these two contrasting masterpieces, both productions were declared winners at the Astor Hotel ceremony, marking the only time in history that the top prize for Best Musical has ever been shared. Id X and Y.", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-06T18:20:19Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Tony", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-06T18:20:25Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Lol", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-06T18:20:27Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Hahahahaha", "role": "chat", "is_correct":